 category="XE" name="SEA" created-by-npl="N"/></sources></patcit><patcit ucid="JP-8119963-A" status="new"><document-id status="new" format="epo"><country>JP</country><doc-number>8119963</doc-number><kind>A</kind></document-id><sources><source category="X" name="SEA" created-by-npl="N"/></sources></patcit><patcit ucid="JP-9241625-A" status="new"><document-id status="new" format="epo"><country>JP</country><doc-number>9241625</doc-number><kind>A</kind></document-id><sources><source category="X" name="SEA" created-by-npl="N"/></sources></patcit></patent-citations><non-patent-citations><nplcit status="new"><text>HIROSHI MIYASAKA ET. AL.: 'Picosecond laser photolysis studies on a photochromic dithenylethene in solution and in crystalline phases.' CHEMICAL PHYSICS LETTERS vol. 269, no. 314, 1997, pages 281 - 285, XP002928058</text><sources><source category="X" name="SEA" created-by-npl="N"/></sources></nplcit><nplcit status="new"><text>See also references of EP     1167487A1</text><sources><source name="SEA" created-by-npl="N"/></sources></nplcit></non-patent-citations></citations></technical-data><parties><applicants><applicant status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>IRIE MASAHIRO</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>JAPAN SCIENCE &amp; TECH CORP</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>IRIE, MASAHIRO</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>JAPAN SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY CORPORATION</name></addressbook></applicant></applicants><inventors><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>IRIE MASAHIRO</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>IRIE, MASAHIRO</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>An organic material undergoing light-induced phase transition comprising a diheteroarylethene compound which in a crystalline state undergoes a photochromic reaction. The diheteroarylethene compound reversibly changes in color upon irradiation with light.  It comes into a liquid phase state in the course of a change from a crystalline open-ring form to a crystalline closed-ring form or in the course of a change from a crystalline closed-ring form to a crystalline open-ring form.  The compound is usable in optical recording media, display panels, sensors, optical switch elements, etc. so as to take advantage of not only the color changing upon light irradiation but differences in refractive index, transmittance, etc. caused by phase changes.</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>L'invention porte sur un matériau organique subissant une transition de phase induite par la lumière et comprenant un composé dihétéroaryléthène qui, à l'état cristallin, subit une réaction photochromique. Le composé dihétéroaryléthène change de couleur de manière réversible lors de l'irradiation de lumière. Il se transforme en phase liquide au cours d'un changement d'une forme cristalline en anneau ouvert en une forme cristalline en anneau fermé, ou au cours d'un changement d'une forme cristalline en anneau fermé en une forme cristalline en anneau ouvert. Le composé peut être utilisé dans un support d'enregistrement optique, des panneaux d'affichage, des capteurs, des éléments de commutation optiques, etc., de façon à bénéficier non seulement du changement de couleur lors de l'irradiation de lumière, mais des différences d'indice de réfraction, de transmittance, etc., dues aux variations de phase.</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to